---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    question: 'Where does the speed test run?',
    answer:
      "It runs in your browser against Cloudflare's global network, using speed.cloudflare.com/__down and speed.cloudflare.com/__up. No app or install is needed.",
  },
  {
    question: 'How are ping and jitter measured?',
    answer:
      'The test starts with one warm-up request, then sends 20 tiny requests. Ping is their median, or middle, round-trip time. Jitter is the average change between consecutive pings.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does the test use larger files?',
    answer:
      'Download sizes ramp from 100 kB to 1 MB, 10 MB, 25 MB, and 100 MB. Upload sizes ramp from 100 kB to 50 MB. The engine skips larger sizes once requests take about one second.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does the test measure loaded latency or packet loss?',
    answer:
      'No. Loaded latency and packet loss are not measured, which keeps a round quick, roughly 20 to 40 seconds.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why can two tests show different numbers?',
    answer:
      'Other devices on the network, Wi-Fi signal, time-of-day congestion, the server route, and browser or device limits can change a test result.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Cloudflare receive my game results?',
    answer:
      'Your game results stay on your device. Cloudflare receives the measurement traffic. See the privacy page for details.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer },
  })),
};
---

<Base
  title="How the Guess the Speed test works"
  description="Learn how the browser speed test measures ping, download speed, upload speed, and game scoring."
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)}></script>
  </Fragment>
  <main class="wrap content-page">
    <span class="round-banner">Under the hood</span>
    <h1>How does the real speed test work?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A real connection check runs right in your browser. It measures ping, download, and upload,
      then turns your guesses into points.
    </p>
    <article class="content-card stk">
      <h2>A real test in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        The test runs in your browser against Cloudflare's global network, using
        <code>speed.cloudflare.com/__down</code> for downloads and
        <code>speed.cloudflare.com/__up</code> for uploads. No app or install is needed.
      </p>

      <h2>Ping and jitter</h2>
      <p>
        The test starts with one warm-up request, then sends 20 tiny requests. Ping is the median,
        or middle, round-trip time. Jitter is the average change between consecutive pings.
      </p>

      <h2>Download and upload ramps</h2>
      <p>
        Download files grow from 100 kB through 1 MB, 10 MB, and 25 MB up to 100 MB. Uploads use
        the same ramp from 100 kB up to 50 MB. When requests take about one second, the engine
        skips bigger files in that direction. Slow connections do not wait forever, and fast
        connections still get pushed.
      </p>

      <h2>How the final speed is chosen</h2>
      <p>
        The final speed is the 90th percentile of the timed requests, and only requests longer
        than 10 ms count. That reflects what the line sustains near its best, not one lucky or
        unlucky burst.
      </p>

      <h2>A quick round, not a network deep dive</h2>
      <p>
        Loaded latency and packet loss are not measured, which keeps a round quick, roughly 20 to
        40 seconds.
      </p>

      <h2>Why does the gauge move that way?</h2>
      <p>
        The gauge scale is not linear. Its marks are 0, 5, 10, 25, 50, 100, 250, 500, and 1000
        Mbps, so both slow and fast lines move the needle. It caps visually at 1000 Mbps, while
        the readout keeps the exact number.
      </p>

      <h2>From guess to points</h2>
      <p>
        For each speed, miss is the gap between the guess and the actual result, divided by the
        actual result. Download and upload misses are averaged. For example, if the test measures
        100 Mbps download and 20 Mbps upload, a guess of 90 and 18 is 10% off on both, for a 10%
        average miss. A lower average miss ranks higher.
      </p>
      <p>
        First place gets 3 points, second gets 2, and third gets 1. A guess within 5% earns a
        bonus point for that speed. See <a href="/how-to-play/">How to play</a> for the full rules
        and tie options.
      </p>

      <h2>Why do two tests give different numbers?</h2>
      <p>
        A speed test is a snapshot of the connection at that moment. Results can shift with other
        devices on the network, Wi-Fi signal, time-of-day congestion, the server route, and
        browser or device limits.
      </p>

      <h2>Where do the results go?</h2>
      <p>
        Game results stay on this device. Cloudflare receives the measurement traffic. Read the
        <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for the details, or visit
        <a href="/internet-speed-101/">Internet speed 101</a> for a friendly guide to Mbps, ping,
        and Wi-Fi.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {questions.map(({ question, answer }) => (
          <section>
            <h3>{question}</h3>
            <p>{answer}</p>
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
